Compositional Safety Assurance under Distributed Authority and Intermittent Communication

Formal Foundations

Ouadi Maakoul, SI Team

---

Abstract

We establish a threshold theory for the compositional safety of critical infrastructure under delayed and intermittent observation, and under distributed decision authority. The principal results are: (i) a counterexample showing that local contract correctness does not entail global safety under a bounded blind window $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$ (Theorem 3.4); (ii) a closed-form sufficient threshold $H_1$ on the initial reserve, together with a matching witness establishing tightness under arbitrary power transitions (Theorems 4.2 and 5.1); (iii) a multi-failure refinement under a bounded-burst contract with an explicit inter-failure gap condition (Theorem 4.4); (iv) a preservation theorem for concurrent decisions under a coherence condition on authority policies, formulated as a recoverable-set inclusion rather than a one-step budget (Theorem 8.1); and (v) a relativised necessity result showing that coherence is architectural rather than logical: under a nominal-precedence axiom the invariant is violated, while under a safe-precedence axiom it is not (Theorems 9.2 and 9.4). All preservation results are bounded to a finite horizon, beyond which an independent architectural property is required (Theorem 6.1). The threshold is stated for an adversarial channel with unconditional bounded recurrence and for an adversarial power contract; both assumptions are made explicit and are shown to be necessary for the quantitative content of the paper.

---

1. Setting

Let $\mathbb{T} = \{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and $\mathbb{T}_{\geq -\delta_{\max}} = \{-\delta_{\max},\ldots,-1\} \cup \mathbb{T}$.

A trace over a finite horizon $N$ is a tuple
\sigma = \big(p(t),\, \hat{p}(t),\, u(t),\, q(t),\, c(t),\, B(t)\big)_{t=0}^{N}.

Parameters. $r_o \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ (nominal production), $c_{\mathrm{nom}} \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ (nominal consumption), $c_{\mathrm{safe}} \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ with $c_{\mathrm{safe}} < c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, $B_{\min} \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ (vital threshold), $B_{\max} \in \mathbb{R}_{> B_{\min}}$ (buffer capacity), $\delta_{\max} \in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$ (maximum delay), $D_{\max} \in \mathbb{N}$ (maximum inter-delivery gap), $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \in \mathbb{N}$ with $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \geq \delta_{\max}$ (maximum recovery horizon). We assume $r_o > c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ wherever multi-failure composition is invoked (§4.4); the single-failure results hold under $r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

The capacity $B_{\max}$ is required for feasibility: every threshold in this paper must be checked against $B_{\max}$, and if it exceeds $B_{\max}$ the architecture is infeasible regardless of the reserve. This condition is stated once in Definition 4.1 and not repeated.

