2. Formal Model

2.1 Physical layer

$p\colon \mathbb{T} \to \{\mathrm{hi},\mathrm{lo}\}$ and
q(t) = F(p(t)) = \begin{cases} r_o & p(t) = \mathrm{hi},\\ 0 & p(t) = \mathrm{lo}. \end{cases} \tag{1}

$G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$. $p(t+1) \in \{\mathrm{hi},\mathrm{lo}\}$ arbitrary. This is the contract under which tightness (Theorem 5.1) is established; it is the most adversarial and hence the most conservative.

$G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}(\tau_{\max}, \gamma_{\min})$. Every maximal $\mathrm{lo}$-interval has length $\leq \tau_{\max}$; consecutive such intervals are separated by $\mathrm{hi}$-intervals of length $\geq \gamma_{\min}$. Evidently $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}} \subsetneq G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$; results proved under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$ are strictly stronger than the corresponding results under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$ on the same parameters, but apply to a smaller mission class.

2.2 Observation layer: intermittent channel

Let $\{c_k\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ be delivery times and $d_k \in [0,\delta_{\max}]$ the delay of the $k$-th message. Let $k^*(t) = \max\{k : c_k \leq t\}$, with $k^*(t)=\varnothing$ if no delivery has occurred. The observed value is
\hat{p}(t) = \begin{cases}
p(c_{k^*(t)} - d_{k^*}) & k^*(t) \neq \varnothing,\\
p(t) & k^*(t) = \varnothing,\ t \leq 0,\\
\hat{p}(t-1) & \text{otherwise (hold-last-value).}
\end{cases} \tag{2}

Channel contract $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$. The channel satisfies:

· (C1) Bounded delay. $d_k \leq \delta_{\max}$ for all $k$.
· (C2) Unconditional bounded recurrence. For every $t \in \mathbb{T}$, some delivery occurs in $(t,\, t + D_{\max} + 1]$. This is a liveness condition, not merely a gap condition: it excludes channel death. Without it, hold-last-value freezes $\hat{p}$ at $\mathrm{hi}$ forever and no finite reserve suffices. The condition is the discrete-time analogue of the maximum allowable transfer interval in networked control, where a dropout bound is always paired with a maximum inter-transmission interval.
· (C3) FIFO with timestamp discard. Messages are delivered in order of dispatch, and any message whose payload is older than a more recently delivered payload is discarded. Without (C3), reordering can resurrect stale $\mathrm{hi}$ values; with (C3), the effective delay of the delivered stream is bounded by $\delta_{\max}$ and the blind-window analysis below is sound.

Lemma 2.1 (Blind window). Under (C1)–(C3), if physical power fails at $t_0$ and has not yet been restored by $t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, where $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} := \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$, then $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for all $t < t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and $\hat{p}(t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) = \mathrm{lo}$.

Proof. The last message showing $\mathrm{hi}$ was dispatched no earlier than $t_0 - \delta_{\max}$ (steady-state convention) and delivered no later than $t_0$; it may be delivered as late as $t_0$ itself, since the payload $p(t_0 - \delta_{\max}) = \mathrm{hi}$ remains valid. By (C2), a delivery occurs in $(t_0,\, t_0 + D_{\max} + 1]$. By (C3), no reordering can insert a stale $\mathrm{hi}$ after this point. The first delivered payload sampled at a time $\geq t_0$ is $\mathrm{lo}$; its dispatch time is $\geq t_0$, its delivery time is $\leq t_0 + D_{\max} + 1$ and $\geq t_0$, and with delay $\leq \delta_{\max}$ the payload time is $\geq t_0$. The latest possible delivery of a $\mathrm{lo}$ payload consistent with all constraints is $t_0 + D_{\max} + \delta_{\max}$. $\square$

2.3 Command, consumption, buffer

u(t) = \pi_{\mathrm{naive}}(\hat{p}(t)) = \begin{cases}\mathrm{normal} & \hat{p}(t)=\mathrm{hi},\\ \mathrm{safe} & \hat{p}(t)=\mathrm{lo},\end{cases} \tag{3}


c(t) = C(u(t)) = \begin{cases} c_{\mathrm{nom}} & u=\mathrm{normal},\\ c_{\mathrm{safe}} & u=\mathrm{safe},\end{cases} \tag{4}


B(t+1) = B(t) + q(t) - c(t), \qquad B(0) = B_0 \in [B_{\min}, B_{\max}]. \tag{5}

2.4 Invariant and admissibility

\mathcal{I}: \quad \forall t \in [0,N],\quad B(t) \geq B_{\min}. \tag{6}

A trace is admissible if it satisfies $G_P \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$, where $G_O$ is (1) and $G_\pi$ is (3).

---

3. Counterexample

Theorem 3.4. There exists an admissible trace under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ violating $\mathcal{I}$.

Proof. Take $r_o = c_{\mathrm{nom}} = 10$, $c_{\mathrm{safe}} = 5$, $B_{\min} = 10$, $\delta_{\max} = 1$, $D_{\max} = 1$, $B_0 = 29$. Set $p(t)=\mathrm{hi}$ for $t<0$ and $p(t)=\mathrm{lo}$ for $t \geq 0$. Choose the channel: a $\mathrm{hi}$ payload dispatched at $t = -2$ with delay $1$ is delivered at $t = -1$; no delivery occurs at $t=0$ (gap of length $1$, admissible under (C2) with $D_{\max}=1$); a $\mathrm{lo}$ payload dispatched at $t=0$ with delay $2$ is delivered at $t=2$. Then $\hat{p}(0)=\hat{p}(1)=\mathrm{hi}$, $\hat{p}(2)=\mathrm{lo}$, so $u(0)=u(1)=\mathrm{normal}$, $u(2)=\mathrm{safe}$. Production is $0$ throughout; consumption is $10,10,5$; buffer is $B(1)=19$, $B(2)=9 < B_{\min}$. All local contracts hold. $\square$

Remark 3.5. The violation occurs exactly at $t = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = 2$. The counterexample is structural: it depends on the separation between physical state and observed state, not on a specification error in any component.

