4. Preservation

4.1 Reserve hypothesis

Definition 4.1 ($H_1$). The parameters satisfy
B_0 \;\geq\; B_{\min} + \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})\, c_{\mathrm{nom}} + \big(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}\big)^{+}\, c_{\mathrm{safe}}, \tag{7}


and the right-hand side is at most $B_{\max}$. We write $\Phi$ for the right-hand side of (7), so $H_1$ is $B_0 \geq \Phi$.

4.2 Single-failure preservation

Theorem 4.2. Suppose $H_1$ holds. Let $\sigma$ be admissible under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ with a single failure interval $[t_0, t_0+\tau)$, $\tau \in [1, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}]$. Then $\mathcal{I}$ holds on $[0, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$.

Proof. Buffer is non-decreasing on $[0,t_0]$ since $p=\mathrm{hi}$, $u=\mathrm{normal}$, $q=r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$; hence $B(t_0) \geq B_0$. Let $\tau' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, \tau)$.

Phase I $[t_0, t_0+\tau')$: by Lemma 2.1, $\hat{p}=\mathrm{hi}$ and $u=\mathrm{normal}$; $q=0$; drain rate $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

Phase II $[t_0+\tau', t_0+\tau)$: if $\tau > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, then $\hat{p}=\mathrm{lo}$, $u=\mathrm{safe}$, $q=0$; drain rate $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

Phase III $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$: $p=\mathrm{hi}$, so $q=r_o$. If $\tau \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, the controller is in $\mathrm{safe}$ throughout Phase III (it has only just learned of the failure that has already resolved); net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}} > 0$. If $\tau < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, then during $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ the controller is still in $\mathrm{normal}$ (it has not yet learned of the failure, which resolved before detection); net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}} \geq 0$. In both sub-cases the buffer is non-decreasing on Phase III. This sub-case distinction is necessary: the rate on Phase III is not uniformly $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

The minimum over $[t_0, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$ is attained at $t_0+\tau$, with
B(t_0+\tau) = B(t_0) - \tau' c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\tau-\tau') c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq B_0 - \tau' c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\tau-\tau') c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


Since $\tau \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $\tau' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},\tau)$, the right-hand side is at least $\Phi \geq B_{\min}$. $\square$

4.3 Tightness

Theorem 5.1. Suppose $B_0 < \Phi$. Then there exists an admissible trace under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ violating $\mathcal{I}$.

Proof. We construct the witness in two cases; the case split is forced by the interaction of delay and recurrence.

Case A ($D_{\max} \geq \delta_{\max}$). Set $p(t)=\mathrm{hi}$ for $t<0$ and $t \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, and $p(t)=\mathrm{lo}$ on $[0,T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$. Drop deliveries on $[0, D_{\max}-1]$; dispatch a $\mathrm{lo}$ payload at $t = D_{\max} - \delta_{\max}$ with delay $\delta_{\max}$, delivering at $t = D_{\max}$. Then the blind window is $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$ and the buffer attains exactly $B_0 - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, which is $< B_{\min}$ by hypothesis.

Case B ($D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$). Do not drop; deliver at every tick with delay $\delta_{\max}$, so the payload at $t$ is $p(t-\delta_{\max})$. Then $\hat{p}(t)=\mathrm{hi}$ for $t < \delta_{\max}$ and $\hat{p}(\delta_{\max})=\mathrm{lo}$; the blind window is $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$ still (the controller learns at $\delta_{\max}$, which is $\leq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$; the extra $D_{\max}$ term in $\Phi$ is not consumed in this construction, but $\Phi$ is by hypothesis below $B_0$, and the buffer at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ under the present construction equals $B_0 - \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$, which is $\leq B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} < B_{\min}$ since $\Phi \geq \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ when $D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$). $\square$

Corollary 5.2. $\Phi$ is the infimum over $B_0$ of values guaranteeing $\mathcal{I}$ over $[0,T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$ under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$, and it is attained.

Remark 5.3. Tightness is established against the adversarial contract $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$ and the naive policy $\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}$. Under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$ the witness of Case A may be excluded by $\gamma_{\min}$, and a buffer-level policy ("ration when $B$ falls below $B_{\min}+\varepsilon$, independently of $\hat{p}$") eliminates the blind-window drain entirely. The threshold $\Phi$ is therefore a worst-case bound, not a lower bound on every safe architecture. This distinction is developed in §10.

4.4 Multi-failure composition

Theorem 4.4. Suppose $H_1$ holds, $r_o > c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, and $\sigma$ is admissible under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}(\tau_{\max},\gamma_{\min}) \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ with $\tau_{\max} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and
\gamma_{\min} \;\geq\; \frac{T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}\, c_{\mathrm{safe}}}{r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}}}. \tag{8}


Then $\mathcal{I}$ holds on every finite horizon.

Proof. By induction on the failure index. Let $t_0^{(i)}$ be the onset of the $i$-th failure. Suppose $B(t_0^{(i)}) \geq \Phi$. Theorem 4.2 gives $B(t) \geq B_{\min}$ throughout the failure and $B(t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i) \geq B_{\min}$. On the recovery interval, the buffer recharges: for the first $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks the controller may still be in $\mathrm{safe}$, with net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$; thereafter it returns to $\mathrm{normal}$, with net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}} > 0$. The gain required to return to $\Phi$ is $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. The gain accumulated during the first $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks is $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}})$; the residual $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}})$ is accumulated at rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}}$. Total recovery length required is therefore
\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} + \frac{T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}})}{r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}}} = \frac{T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}}{r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}}},


which is (8). $\square$

Remark 4.5. Under $r_o = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ condition (8) is infinite: no finite inter-failure gap suffices, and the multi-failure case is genuinely unsafe. Condition (8) is sufficient but conservative: it requires full recharge to $\Phi$ before the next failure, whereas the next failure only requires a security level — the minimum reserve at onset such that the remaining sequence is safe. Tracking this level yields a fixpoint condition weaker than (8) when $r_o \gg c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

