5. Bounded Validity

Theorem 6.1. Let $H_1$ hold. For every $B_0 \geq \Phi$ there exists $N > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and an admissible failure pattern such that $\mathcal{I}$ is violated on $[0,N]$.

Proof. Take $p(t)=\mathrm{lo}$ for all $t \geq 0$. Then $B(t) = B_0 - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (t-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ for $t \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, strictly decreasing and unbounded below. Choose $t^* = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} + (B_0 - B_{\min} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}})/c_{\mathrm{safe}} + 1$; then $B(t^*) < B_{\min}$ and $t^* > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ when $B_0$ is large. $\square$

Note that $t^*$ depends on $B_0 - B_{\min}$, not on $B_{\min}$ alone.

Corollary 6.2. Indefinite preservation under $H_1$ requires at least one of: (R) redundancy, (E) evacuation by $t^*$, or (Q) controlled rationing below $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ indefinitely. None is derivable from the composition.

---

6. Sufficient Alternatives

$H_2$ (local sensor). A local observation channel with delay bound $\delta_{\mathrm{loc}}$ and recurrence bound $D_{\mathrm{loc}}$ replaces the remote channel for safety-critical commands. Then $H_1$ holds with $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ replaced by $\delta_{\mathrm{loc}} + D_{\mathrm{loc}}$. The hypothesis is bounded-delay local observation, not zero-delay observation; the latter is a limiting case and should not be confused with the hypothesis actually used.

$H_3$ (minimum failure duration). Every $\mathrm{lo}$-interval has length at least $\tau_{\min} \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. Under this hypothesis, the controller necessarily receives a $\mathrm{lo}$ payload before restoration, so it never acts on a false recovery; the recovery phase is uniformly in $\mathrm{safe}$ mode and the rate on Phase III is uniformly $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. The quantitative threshold is unchanged, but the Phase-III case split of Theorem 4.2 disappears and the multi-failure condition (8) can be sharpened.

Remark 6.3. A hypothesis of $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell time alone does not contract the blind window. A delivered $\mathrm{hi}$ payload may be $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ old at the moment of failure, regardless of how long $\mathrm{hi}$ persisted before that failure. What removes the $D_{\max}$ term is a minimum $\mathrm{lo}$-duration, not a minimum $\mathrm{hi}$-duration. The earlier formulation of $H_3$ in terms of $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell was incorrect and is here replaced by $H_3$ above.

