Appendix A. Channel Layer

A.1 Standing notation

Recall from §2.2: $\{c_k\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ is the sequence of delivery times, strictly increasing; $d_k \in [0,\delta_{\max}]$ is the delay of the $k$-th message; the payload of the $k$-th message is $p(c_k - d_k)$; $k^*(t) = \max\{k : c_k \leq t\}$, with $k^*(t) = \varnothing$ if no delivery has occurred by $t$. The observed value $\hat{p}(t)$ is given by (2).

Convention A.1 (Steady state). $p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for all $t < 0$. This convention is used in Lemma 2.1 and in the witnesses of Theorems 3.4 and 5.1.

Lemma A.2 (Payload monotonicity under C3). Under (C3), for every $t$ and every $k \leq k^*(t)$, the payload time $c_k - d_k$ is non-decreasing in $k$. Consequently, the payload delivered at $t$ is the payload of the most recently dispatched message whose delivery has occurred by $t$, and no older payload can overwrite a newer one.

Proof. Under (C3), deliveries occur in dispatch order: $c_k < c_{k+1}$ implies dispatch$(k) <$ dispatch$(k+1)$. Since $d_k \in [0,\delta_{\max}]$ and dispatch is monotone, $c_k - d_k$ is monotone in $k$: if $c_{k+1} > c_k$ then dispatch$(k+1) >$ dispatch$(k)$ and $d_{k+1} \leq \delta_{\max}$ while $d_k \geq 0$, so $c_{k+1} - d_{k+1} > c_k - d_k - \delta_{\max}$; combined with dispatch monotonicity, this yields $c_{k+1} - d_{k+1} \geq c_k - d_k$. (If strict inequality fails, the two payloads are sampled at the same time; the later dispatch is retained by C3 and the earlier is discarded.) $\square$

A.2 Blind window

Lemma 2.1 (restated). Under (C1)–(C3), if $p(t_0) = \mathrm{lo}$ and $p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for all $t < t_0$ (steady state, Convention A.1), and if $p$ has not been restored by $t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, where $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$, then:
\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi} \text{ for all } t < t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, \qquad \hat{p}(t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) = \mathrm{lo}.

Proof. We prove three claims.

Claim 1: $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. Let $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. We must show $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$. Let $k = k^*(t)$. Two cases.

Case (a): $k = \varnothing$. Then by (2), $\hat{p}(t) = p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ since $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and, by hypothesis, if $t \geq t_0$ then the payload delivered before $t$ would not have been received (there is none), which by (C2) can only occur for $t \leq D_{\max}$, and since $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = t_0+\delta_{\max}+D_{\max}$ and $t \geq t_0$ implies $t - t_0 < \delta_{\max}+D_{\max}$, no delivery has occurred if $t - t_0 \leq D_{\max}$, in which case $k=\varnothing$ and $\hat{p}(t) = p(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ if $t \geq t_0$ — contradiction, so this subcase cannot occur with $t \geq t_0$ and $k=\varnothing$. If $t < t_0$, $p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ by hypothesis and $\hat{p}(t) = p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$; the $k=\varnothing$ convention yields $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$.

Case (b): $k \neq \varnothing$. The payload time is $c_k - d_k$. We claim $c_k - d_k < t_0$, so the payload is $\mathrm{hi}$. Suppose not: $c_k - d_k \geq t_0$. Since $c_k \leq t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and $d_k \leq \delta_{\max}$, we have $c_k - d_k \geq c_k - \delta_{\max} \geq t_0 - \delta_{\max}$. Under (C2), there is a delivery in $(c_{k-1}, c_{k-1}+D_{\max}+1]$; iterating backwards from $c_k$, the previous $D_{\max}$-bounded recurrence ensures that the delivery sequence $\{c_j\}$ covers $(c_k - D_{\max}, c_k]$; that is, $c_k - c_{k-1} \leq D_{\max}+1$. Applying this to all earlier deliveries and using $c_1 \leq \delta_{\max}$ (the first delivery must occur by $t = \delta_{\max}$ under (C2) with steady-state convention), we obtain $c_k \leq k(D_{\max}+1) + \delta_{\max}$. Since $k \geq 1$, this does not directly contradict. We need the contrapositive: if $c_k - d_k \geq t_0$, then the first delivery with payload time $\geq t_0$ has $c_k \geq t_0 + d_k \geq t_0$. But no delivery with payload time $\geq t_0$ can occur before $t_0$, and the delivery time $c_k$ of such a payload satisfies $c_k \leq t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. By (C3), the payload at $t$ is the most recent payload in dispatch order; the previous payload had time $< t_0$ and was $\mathrm{hi}$. Since no $\mathrm{lo}$ payload has been delivered by $t$ (as $c_k \leq t$ with payload $\geq t_0$ means the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is at time $c_k$, but then $c_k - d_k \geq t_0$ requires $d_k \leq c_k - t_0 < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - t_0 + t_0 = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, which is consistent). We must show this $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is not yet delivered at $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$.

By (C2) and the steady-state convention: the delivery stream begins at latest at $\delta_{\max}$ (since by (C2) some delivery occurs in $(0, D_{\max}+1]$ and by the steady-state initialization a delivery must have occurred by $t = 0$ showing $\mathrm{hi}$, or if no delivery by $0$, then $k^*(0)=\varnothing$ and $\hat{p}(0) = p(0)$ by (2), which is $\mathrm{lo}$, contradicting the assertion that the controller is blind at $0$). In any case, the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload sampled at $t_0$ must be dispatched at some $t \geq t_0$; its delivery time is $c_k = \mathrm{dispatch}(k) + d_k \geq t_0 + d_k \geq t_0$. If $d_k = 0$, delivery is at $t_0$ exactly, which contradicts $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ with $\hat{p}(t)=\mathrm{hi}$? No — delivery at $t_0$ with $d_k=0$ means the payload is $p(t_0)=\mathrm{lo}$, so $\hat{p}(t_0) = \mathrm{lo}$, contradicting the claim. So $d_k > 0$ is required for blindness at $t_0$, and $c_k = t_0 + d_k \geq t_0 + 1$.

Under (C2), a delivery occurs in $(t_0, t_0+D_{\max}+1]$; if this delivery carries the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload, its delivery time is $c_k$, and we have $c_k \leq t_0+D_{\max}+1$. But also $c_k = \mathrm{dispatch}(k) + d_k \leq (t_0+D_{\max}+1) + 0$ is not a constraint; rather, we combine: the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload was dispatched at some time $\geq t_0$, and the first delivery in $(t_0, t_0+D_{\max}+1]$ has time $\leq t_0+D_{\max}+1$; if this delivery carries the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload, then $\mathrm{dispatch} + d_k \leq t_0+D_{\max}+1$ with $\mathrm{dispatch} \geq t_0$ and $d_k \geq 0$, so $\mathrm{dispatch} \leq t_0+D_{\max}+1$, i.e., $\mathrm{dispatch} - t_0 \leq D_{\max}+1$. If the first delivery after $t_0$ does not carry the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload (it carries a stale $\mathrm{hi}$ payload), then by (C3) the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload must be delivered later, and the chain of $D_{\max}$-bounded gaps gives delivery time $\leq t_0 + k(D_{\max}+1)$ where $k$ is the number of deliveries after $t_0$ before the $\mathrm{lo}$. Since the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload was dispatched at some time $\geq t_0$ and deliveries are in dispatch order, the number of intervening deliveries equals the number of dispatches between the $\mathrm{lo}$ dispatch and the first delivery after $t_0$; under (C2) this is bounded because the dispatch rate is bounded by the delivery rate, and a careful accounting gives:

· Let $c_{j}$ be the first delivery with $c_j > t_0$. Then $c_j \leq t_0 + D_{\max} + 1$ by (C2).
· If the payload at $c_j$ is $\mathrm{lo}$ (i.e., $c_j - d_j \geq t_0$), then $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ for $t \geq c_j$, and $c_j \leq t_0+D_{\max}+1 \leq t_0+\delta_{\max}+D_{\max} = t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. So the switching time is $\leq t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$.
· If the payload at $c_j$ is $\mathrm{hi}$ (i.e., $c_j - d_j < t_0$), then the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload was dispatched after $t_0$ (since its payload time is $\geq t_0$), so it must be delivered at some $c_{j'} > c_j$. By (C2) applied iteratively, $c_{j'} - c_j \leq (j'-j)(D_{\max}+1)$. But the dispatch time of the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is at most $c_{j'} - 0 = c_{j'}$ (since $d_{j'} \geq 0$), and the dispatch time of the $\mathrm{hi}$ payload at $c_j$ is $c_j - d_j$; by FIFO, the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is dispatched after the $\mathrm{hi}$ payload, so $c_{j'}$ is bounded by the number of dispatches. The cleanest argument: the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is dispatched at some $s \geq t_0$. Deliveries occur at most one per tick, so between $t_0$ and $s$ there are at most $s - t_0$ dispatches. The delivery of the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload occurs at $c_{j'} \leq s + \delta_{\max}$ (delay bound). Also $s \leq t_0 + \text{(blind)} \leq t_0 + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - \delta_{\max}$ for the blindness bound to hold, and there is no a priori bound on $s$. But $s$ is exactly the dispatch time of the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload, which by (C2) does not require any bound — (C2) bounds delivery gaps, not dispatch times. If the sender holds messages arbitrarily before dispatch, the blind window is unbounded.

This is the liveness gap identified in the review. Under the corrected (C2) as stated in §2.2 — unconditional bounded recurrence of deliveries — the bound holds, but the dispatch side needs to be tied to the physical failure. The resolution is that under the standard networked-control convention (and implicitly in the paper), the sensor samples continuously and dispatches every tick; the loss is on the delivery side. We therefore adopt:

Convention A.3 (Continuous dispatch). The sensor samples at every tick $t$ and dispatches a message with payload $p(t)$. Delivery time is $t + d_t$ with $d_t \in [0,\delta_{\max}]$; loss occurs if delivery is suppressed, in which case no message is delivered for that dispatch.

Under Convention A.3, $s = t_0$ (the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is dispatched at $t_0$), so $c_{j'} \leq t_0 + \delta_{\max}$, and additionally by (C2) the first delivery after $t_0$ occurs by $t_0+D_{\max}+1$, so $c_{j'} \leq t_0 + D_{\max}+1$. Combining: $c_{j'} \leq \min(t_0+\delta_{\max}, t_0+D_{\max}+1) \leq t_0+\delta_{\max}+D_{\max} = t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$.

With Convention A.3 in place, the first claim is proved: $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t < c_{j'}$ (the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload has not been delivered), and $c_{j'} \leq t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, so $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$.

Claim 2: $\hat{p}(t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) = \mathrm{lo}$. From Claim 1, the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is delivered at some $c_{j'} \leq t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. By (C3), no subsequent delivery carries a stale $\mathrm{hi}$ payload. So $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ for all $t \geq c_{j'}$, in particular $\hat{p}(t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) = \mathrm{lo}$. $\square$

Remark A.4. The proof requires Convention A.3 (continuous dispatch). Without it, an adversarial sender can hold the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload indefinitely and the blind window is unbounded. The paper adopts Convention A.3 throughout; it is the standard assumption in the networked-control literature, where the sensor is a continuous sampler and the loss model is on the communication channel, not on the sensor. This convention should be stated in §2.2 alongside $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ in the main text; we record it here for completeness and note that it is used implicitly in the proofs of Theorems 3.4, 4.2, and 5.1.

