Appendix C. Single-Failure Preservation and Tightness

C.1 Phase decomposition

Lemma C.1 (Phase structure). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.2 with a single failure interval $[t_0, t_0+\tau)$ and $\tau' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, \tau)$:

(i) On $[t_0, t_0+\tau')$: $\hat{p} = \mathrm{hi}$, $u = \mathrm{normal}$, $q = 0$, $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

(ii) On $[t_0+\tau', t_0+\tau)$ (non-empty iff $\tau > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$): $\hat{p} = \mathrm{lo}$, $u = \mathrm{safe}$, $q = 0$, $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

(iii) On $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$: $p = \mathrm{hi}$, $q = r_o$; if $\tau \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, then $u = \mathrm{safe}$, $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$; if $\tau < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, then $u = \mathrm{normal}$ on $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ and $u = \mathrm{safe}$ on $[t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ (the latter interval is non-empty).

Proof. (i) By Lemma 2.1 applied to the failure onset $t_0$: for $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$; in particular on $[t_0, t_0+\tau') \subseteq [t_0, t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$. By (3), $u = \mathrm{normal}$; by (1), $q = 0$ (since $p = \mathrm{lo}$ on $[t_0, t_0+\tau)$ and $\tau' \leq \tau$); by (4), $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

(ii) By (i), $\tau' = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} < \tau$ in this case. For $t \in [t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, t_0+\tau)$, Lemma 2.1 gives $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ (restoration is at $t_0+\tau$, after $t$, so $p = \mathrm{lo}$ throughout and the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload received at $t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ persists). By (3), $u = \mathrm{safe}$; by (1), $q = 0$; by (4), $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

(iii) $p = \mathrm{hi}$ on $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ by hypothesis. $q = r_o$ by (1). For the command: if $\tau \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, then at $t = t_0+\tau \geq t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ the controller has received the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload (Lemma 2.1) and holds $\hat{p} = \mathrm{lo}$ until it receives a $\mathrm{hi}$ payload from the restoration. The first restoration payload is sampled at $t_0+\tau$ (under Convention A.3) and delivered at $t_0+\tau+d \leq t_0+\tau+\delta_{\max} \leq t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. So on $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$, the controller may or may not receive the restoration $\mathrm{hi}$; in either case $u = \mathrm{safe}$ is the weaker assumption, and by (4) $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ or $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ depending on whether the restoration payload has arrived. Since $r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}} \geq c_{\mathrm{safe}}$, both are consistent with the claimed non-decrease of $B$ (see the proof of Theorem 4.2). If $\tau < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, the controller has not yet received the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload (by Lemma 2.1 applied to the failure onset $t_0$: $t < t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ implies $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$). Hence $u = \mathrm{normal}$ and $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ on $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$; at $t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload arrives, and the controller switches to $\mathrm{safe}$ on $[t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$, during which $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. $\square$

C.2 Preservation

Theorem 4.2 (restated). Suppose $H_1$ holds. Let $\sigma$ be admissible under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ with a single failure interval $[t_0, t_0+\tau)$, $\tau \in [1, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}]$. Then $\mathcal{I}$ holds on $[0, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$.

Proof. Pre-failure phase. For $t \leq t_0$, $p = \mathrm{hi}$ so $q = r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$. The controller observes $\hat{p} = \mathrm{hi}$ (steady state, no failure yet in the observed stream at $t_0$; formally, by Convention A.3 and Lemma A.2, $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t \leq t_0$), so $u = \mathrm{normal}$ and $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$. Net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}} \geq 0$; hence $B(t) \geq B(t')$ for $t \geq t'$ in $[0, t_0]$, and in particular $B(t_0) \geq B_0$.

Failure phase. By Lemma C.1, split into three sub-phases:

· Phase I: drain rate $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ over $\tau' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, \tau)$ ticks.
· Phase II: drain rate $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ over $(\tau - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+$ ticks.
· Phase III: net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ over $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks in the $\tau \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ case, or (net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ over $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - \tau$ ticks) + (net rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ over $\tau$ ticks) in the $\tau < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ case. In the first case the net rate is positive; in the second case both net rates are non-negative ($r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ by hypothesis in §1, and $c_{\mathrm{nom}} > c_{\mathrm{safe}}$). In all cases the buffer is non-decreasing on Phase III.

The minimum over $[t_0, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$ is attained at $t = t_0+\tau$ (the end of the failure, before restoration), with
B(t_0+\tau) = B(t_0) - \tau' c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\tau - \tau') c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


Since $B(t_0) \geq B_0$, $\tau' \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, and $\tau - \tau' = (\tau - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+$,
B(t_0+\tau) \geq B_0 - \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},\tau) c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


Since $\tau \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, this is $\geq B_0 - \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Phi \geq B_{\min}$ by $H_1$. $\square$

C.3 Tightness

Theorem 5.1 (restated). Suppose $B_0 < \Phi$. Then there exists an admissible trace under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ violating $\mathcal{I}$.

Proof. We construct the trace in two cases, as claimed in the main text. Let the failure be $p(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ on $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$ and $p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ elsewhere.

Case A: $D_{\max} \geq \delta_{\max}$.

Channel schedule: suppress all deliveries on $[0, D_{\max}-1]$; dispatch payload $p(D_{\max}-\delta_{\max})$ at time $D_{\max}-\delta_{\max}$ with delay $\delta_{\max}$, so it is delivered at $t = D_{\max}$ with payload $p(D_{\max}-\delta_{\max})$. Since $D_{\max}-\delta_{\max} \geq 0$ (Case A), the payload is $\mathrm{lo}$ if $D_{\max}-\delta_{\max} < T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $\mathrm{hi}$ otherwise. To ensure the payload is $\mathrm{lo}$, choose $D_{\max} - \delta_{\max} < T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$; if $D_{\max}-\delta_{\max} \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, then $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \geq \delta_{\max} + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, so $\Phi = B_{\min} + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ (the second term of (7) vanishes), and the witness uses $\tau = T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ with continuous nominal drain for all $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ ticks, which is achieved by simply never delivering any $\mathrm{lo}$ payload within $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$ — the controller stays in $\mathrm{normal}$ mode throughout, draining at $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, and $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} = \Phi - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}) + B_{\min} - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} \cdot 0$... let me recompute. If $B_0 < \Phi = B_{\min} + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, then $B_0 - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} < B_{\min}$, and the invariant is violated at $t = T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ under continuous nominal drain. This is the degenerate case; it is covered.

Otherwise, $D_{\max}-\delta_{\max} < T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and the payload delivered at $t = D_{\max}$ is $\mathrm{lo}$. Let $c_1 < c_2 < \cdots$ be the deliveries. Under Convention A.3, at each $t \in [0, D_{\max}-1]$, if a dispatch occurs (at every $t$), delivery is suppressed; the last delivery before $t=0$ carries a $\mathrm{hi}$ payload and is held by (2). At $t = D_{\max}$, the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload arrives. For $t \geq D_{\max}$, deliveries occur at every dispatch (we choose no loss for $t \geq D_{\max}$; this is admissible under (C2) since $D_{\max}+1$ is the maximum gap and a gap of $1$ is fine).

Check (C2): the gap between the last delivery before $0$ (say at $t = -1$ with payload $p(-2)=\mathrm{hi}$; suppressed dispatches at $t \geq 0$ until $D_{\max}-1$) and $c_1 = D_{\max}$ is $D_{\max}+1$. ✓.

Observation trace: $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ on $[0, D_{\max})$ (hold-last-value), $\hat{p}(D_{\max}) = \mathrm{lo}$.

Command trace: $u(t) = \mathrm{normal}$ on $[0, D_{\max})$, $u(t) = \mathrm{safe}$ on $[D_{\max}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$.

Consumption: $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ on $[0, D_{\max})$, $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ on $[D_{\max}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$, and $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ on $[T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ (controller still in $\mathrm{safe}$ mode; the restoration $\mathrm{hi}$ payload arrives with delay, but on this interval either $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ or $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ is possible and $r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ ensures non-decrease).

Production: $q = 0$ on $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$, $q = r_o$ on $[T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$.

Buffer at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$:
B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - D_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} - D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


We need to relate this to $\Phi$. In Case A, $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max}+D_{\max}$. Two sub-cases:

Sub-case A.1: $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$. Then $\Phi = B_{\min} + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. We have $D_{\max} \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, so $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - D_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. This equals $B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} + (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. Rearranging: $= B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} + (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}-D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}-D_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} = B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} + (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}-D_{\max})(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}})$. Since $c_{\mathrm{nom}} > c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ and $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \geq D_{\max}$, this is $\geq B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min}$. Since $B_0 < \Phi$, we have $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) < B_{\min}$. ✓

Sub-case A.2: $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$. Then $\Phi = B_{\min} + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, and the second term of (7) vanishes. The witness should drain at $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ for all $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ ticks. Achieve this by suppressing all deliveries until $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-1$ (a gap of $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, which requires $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \leq D_{\max}+1$; since $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max}+D_{\max} > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, we have $D_{\max} \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} - \delta_{\max}+1$, and if $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \leq D_{\max}+1$ the suppression is admissible). If $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} > D_{\max}+1$, then $D_{\max} < T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} - 1 \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - 1 - \delta_{\max}$, and we are not in sub-case A.2. So sub-case A.2 implies $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} \leq D_{\max}+1$, and suppression until $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-1$ is admissible. Then $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} = B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} < B_{\min}$. ✓

Case B: $D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$.

Channel schedule: continuous dispatch with maximal delay $\delta_{\max}$; no loss. Deliveries at every $t \geq \delta_{\max}$ (the payload dispatched at $t-\delta_{\max}$ delivered at $t$); deliveries for $t < \delta_{\max}$ carry steady-state payloads (dispatch times $t-\delta_{\max} < 0$, so $p = \mathrm{hi}$).

Check (C1): $d = \delta_{\max}$. ✓
Check (C2): delivery gap $1 \leq D_{\max}+1$ (trivially, since $D_{\max} \geq 0$). ✓
Check (C3): FIFO trivially. ✓

Observation trace: $\hat{p}(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t < \delta_{\max}$ (payloads $p(t-\delta_{\max}) = \mathrm{hi}$ for $t-\delta_{\max} < 0$); $\hat{p}(\delta_{\max}) = p(0) = \mathrm{lo}$.

Command trace: $u(t) = \mathrm{normal}$ on $[0, \delta_{\max})$, $u(t) = \mathrm{safe}$ on $[\delta_{\max}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$.

Consumption: $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ on $[0, \delta_{\max})$, $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ on $[\delta_{\max}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$.

Buffer at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$:
B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


In Case B, $D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$ so $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max} + D_{\max}$ and $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - \delta_{\max} = D_{\max} > 0$. Thus
B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min} + (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}-\delta_{\max})(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) \cdot \mathbf{1}[\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}] + \ldots


Actually, in Case B, we have $\Phi = B_{\min} + \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

Sub-case B.1: $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$. Then $\Phi = B_{\min} + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. We have $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. Subtract: $B_0 - B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$; $\Phi - B_{\min} = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. Difference: $(\Phi - B_{\min}) - (B_0 - B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})) = (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (\delta_{\max}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} = D_{\max}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) > 0$. So $B_0 - B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) < \Phi - B_{\min}$, i.e., $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) > B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min}$. Since $B_0 < \Phi$, this gives $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) > B_{\min}$ — the inequality goes the wrong way for a violation. Hmm.

The issue is that in Case B, the blind window bound $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max}+D_{\max}$ is not attained by the witness: the witness has blind window $\delta_{\max} < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. The drain is less than the worst case, so the witness does not violate at $B_0 < \Phi$ unless $B_0 < B_{\min} + \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$.

This means the main-text claim that the witness violates at $B_0 < \Phi$ in Case B is not correct as stated. The correct statement is:

Corrected Theorem 5.1. Suppose $B_0 < \Phi$. Then there exists an admissible trace under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ violating $\mathcal{I}$, provided the adversarial channel can realize the blind window $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ — i.e., provided the loss schedule of Case A is realizable, which requires $D_{\max} \geq 1$ or the existence of a delivery pattern achieving $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ under (C2).

In Case B, $\Phi$ includes a penalty $D_{\max}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}})$ that the witness cannot realize: it is a conservative bound with slack $D_{\max}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}})$ in Case B.

Corollary 5.2 (corrected). Under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$ and $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$, the tight threshold is
\Phi^{\mathrm{tight}} = B_{\min} + \delta_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\delta_{\max})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}}


when $D_{\max} = 0$, and
\Phi^{\mathrm{tight}} = B_{\min} + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}}


when $D_{\max} \geq 1$; more precisely, when the channel can produce a delivery gap of length $D_{\max}+1$ that straddles the failure onset. $\Phi$ in (7) is exact when $D_{\max} \geq 1$; when $D_{\max} = 0$, $\Phi$ reduces to $\Phi^{\mathrm{tight}}$ because $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} = \delta_{\max}$. The Case-B situation $0 < D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$ has a slack of $D_{\max}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}})$ in $\Phi$ that no witness can attain, because the channel cannot sustain a delivery gap $D_{\max}+1$ across the failure onset while still delivering at delay $\delta_{\max}$ — these two requirements are compatible only when the gap is filled by a dispatch that is delivered but is stale enough to be discarded by C3.

Resolution. The slack is eliminated by noting that the bound $\Phi$ is what the theorem proves; tightness at $\Phi$ is established for $D_{\max} \geq 1$ (Case A) and for $D_{\max} = 0$ (Case B, where the slack vanishes). For $0 < D_{\max} < \delta_{\max}$, $\Phi$ is a conservative threshold and the tight threshold is the smaller quantity. This is a genuine correction to the main text, and the tightness statement should be read as $D_{\max} \notin (0, \delta_{\max})$; for the intermediate regime, $\Phi$ is sufficient and $\Phi - D_{\max}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}})$ is necessary.

$\square$

Remark C.2. The main text's Theorem 5.1 asserts tightness in Case B by the argument that $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) \leq B_0 - \Phi + B_{\min}$ — that argument is incorrect because it forgets that the witness's blind window in Case B is $\delta_{\max}$, not $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. The corrected statement above replaces the claim. This is a substantive correction to the main text and the theorem statement in §4.3 should be revised accordingly.

