Appendix D. Multi-Failure Preservation

Theorem 4.4 (restated). Suppose $H_1$ holds, $r_o > c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, and $\sigma$ is admissible under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}(\tau_{\max},\gamma_{\min}) \wedge G_O \wedge G_\pi \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ with $\tau_{\max} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and (8). Then $\mathcal{I}$ holds on every finite horizon.

Proof. Let $t_0^{(1)} < t_0^{(2)} < \cdots$ be the failure onsets, and $\tau_i$ the duration of the $i$-th failure. By $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$, $\tau_i \leq \tau_{\max} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, and $t_0^{(i+1)} - (t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i) \geq \gamma_{\min}$.

Claim: $B(t_0^{(i)}) \geq \Phi$ for all $i$. By induction on $i$.

Base ($i=1$). By $H_1$, $B_0 \geq \Phi$. If $t_0^{(1)} = 0$, done. If $t_0^{(1)} > 0$, then $p = \mathrm{hi}$ on $[0, t_0^{(1)})$, so $q = r_o \geq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ and $B$ is non-decreasing; $B(t_0^{(1)}) \geq B_0 \geq \Phi$. ✓

Step. Suppose $B(t_0^{(i)}) \geq \Phi$. By Theorem 4.2, $B(t) \geq B_{\min}$ on $[t_0^{(i)}, t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$, and in particular $B(t) \geq B_{\min}$ throughout the failure and on the first $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks of recovery. We must show $B(t_0^{(i+1)}) \geq \Phi$. It suffices to show that the buffer recovers to $\Phi$ within $\gamma_{\min}$ ticks of the failure end, since the recovery interval length is $\geq \gamma_{\min}$.

Recovery phase. On $[t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i, t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$, the controller may be in $\mathrm{safe}$ mode (it learned of the failure at $t_0^{(i)}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, which is after the failure end when $\tau_i < \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$; and the restoration payload is delayed). In the worst case, $u = \mathrm{safe}$ throughout, so $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ and the net rate is $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. After $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, the controller has received the restoration payload (by Lemma 2.1 applied to the restoration, or by the simpler observation that the restoration payload is dispatched at $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i$ under Convention A.3 and delivered by $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i+\delta_{\max} \leq t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$), so $u = \mathrm{normal}$ and $c = c_{\mathrm{nom}}$; the net rate is $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}} > 0$.

Minimum gain needed. The buffer at $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i$ is at least $B_{\min}$ (by Theorem 4.2). To reach $\Phi$, the gain required is $\Phi - B_{\min}$. But this is an over-estimate: the buffer at $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i$ is at least $B(t_0^{(i)}) - \tau_i' c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\tau_i-\tau_i') c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ where $\tau_i' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},\tau_i)$. Since $B(t_0^{(i)}) \geq \Phi$, the buffer at $t_0^{(i)}+\tau_i$ is at least
\Phi - \tau_i' c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (\tau_i-\tau_i') c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq \Phi - \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}.


If we allow the worst-case drain $\tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ (which is conservative since $\tau_i' \leq \tau_i \leq \tau_{\max}$), the gain needed to return to $\Phi$ is at most $\tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

Gain accumulated. Over the first $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks of recovery, gain is $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}})$. Over the remaining $\gamma_{\min} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks, gain is $(\gamma_{\min}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}})$. Total:
G(\gamma_{\min}) = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + (\gamma_{\min}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}}).


We need $G(\gamma_{\min}) \geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$. By (8), $\gamma_{\min} \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}/(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}})$, so
G(\gamma_{\min}) \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + \left(\frac{T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}}{r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}\right)(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}}).


Expanding:
= \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(r_o-c_{\mathrm{nom}})
= \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


We need this $\geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$. From (7), $\Phi - B_{\min} = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})^+ c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ (when $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$). So $G(\gamma_{\min}) \geq \Phi - B_{\min} \geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$? No — $\Phi - B_{\min} \geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ requires $\Phi \geq B_{\min} + \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, which holds since $\tau_{\max} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $\Phi - B_{\min} \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$? The last inequality: since $\tau_{\max} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{nom}} > c_{\mathrm{safe}}$, $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} = T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. This does not immediately give $\geq \tau_{\max} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$.

We need a direct argument. From (8), the recovery gain $G(\gamma_{\min})$ satisfies
G(\gamma_{\min}) = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}}.


And the buffer deficit at the failure end is at most $\tau_i' c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (\tau_i-\tau_i') c_{\mathrm{safe}} \leq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ (since $\tau_i \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $\tau_i' = \min(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}},\tau_i)$). The difference is
G(\gamma_{\min}) - (\text{deficit}) \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} = 0.


So the gain is exactly sufficient. More carefully: the deficit is at most $\tau_i' c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (\tau_i-\tau_i') c_{\mathrm{safe}}$, and we need $G(\gamma_{\min}) \geq$ deficit. The deficit is maximized when $\tau_i = T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, giving $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ (if $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$) or $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ (if $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$). In the latter case $G(\gamma_{\min}) = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) + T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, so the gain exceeds the deficit. In the former case $G(\gamma_{\min}) = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} =$ deficit, so the gain exactly matches. In both cases $B(t_0^{(i+1)}) \geq \Phi$. ✓

Therefore $B(t_0^{(i+1)}) \geq \Phi$, completing the induction. By Theorem 4.2 applied at each failure, the invariant holds throughout. $\square$

Correction D.1. The above proof shows that condition (8) is exactly sufficient for the induction to close, in the worst case $\tau_i = T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$ and $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$. It is not merely sufficient but tight for the multi-failure composition under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$ with $\gamma_{\min}$ equal to the right-hand side of (8). The main text's Remark 4.5 says the condition is "conservative"; this is correct only for $r_o \gg c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ where the gain in the recovery phase is strictly greater than the deficit; in the worst case it is exact.

