Appendix E. Bounded Validity and Alternatives

Theorem 6.1 (restated). Let $H_1$ hold. For every $B_0 \geq \Phi$ there exists $N > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and an admissible failure pattern such that $\mathcal{I}$ is violated on $[0,N]$.

Proof. Take $p(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ for all $t \geq 0$; the failure is never restored. Then for $t \geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, the controller has learned of the failure (Lemma 2.1) and $u = \mathrm{safe}$; $q = 0$; $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$. The buffer evolves as
B(t) = B(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) - (t - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}, \qquad B(\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) = B_0 - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}.


Set $t^* = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} + \lceil (B_0 - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - B_{\min})/c_{\mathrm{safe}} \rceil + 1$. Then $B(t^*) < B_{\min}$ and $t^* > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ provided $B_0 - B_{\min} > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ (which is $\geq \Phi - B_{\min}$ up to the case distinction of (7); it holds when $B_0 \geq \Phi$ and $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, and it holds a fortiori when $B_0$ is larger). Set $N = t^*$. The invariant is violated at $t^*$. $\square$

Note that $t^*$ depends on $B_0 - B_{\min}$, not on $B_{\min}$ alone; this corrects the main text.

Lemma E.1 ($H_2$ reduction). Under $H_2$, the threshold (7) holds with $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ replaced by $\Delta_{\mathrm{loc}} = \delta_{\mathrm{loc}} + D_{\mathrm{loc}}$.

Proof. Identical to Theorem 4.2 with the local channel replacing the remote channel for the command decision. The observation $\hat{p}_{\mathrm{loc}}$ is subject to its own (C1)–(C3) with parameters $(\delta_{\mathrm{loc}}, D_{\mathrm{loc}})$, and Lemma 2.1 applies. $\square$

Lemma E.2 ($H_3$ non-contraction of the blind window). Under a $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell hypothesis of the form "if $p(t)=\mathrm{hi}$ then $p(t')=\mathrm{hi}$ for $t' \in [t, t+\delta_{\max})$", the blind window is not contracted; in particular, there exists a trace consistent with the $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell and with $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ in which the controller acts on a stale $\mathrm{hi}$ for $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks after the failure onset.

Proof. Suppose $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell holds. Let the failure occur at $t_0$. The last $\mathrm{hi}$ payload delivered before $t_0$ was sampled at some time $s < t_0$ and may be delivered as late as $t_0$ (if delayed by $\delta_{\max}$) or held thereafter under a delivery gap of length $D_{\max}+1$; in either case, the controller sees $\mathrm{hi}$ for $t \in [t_0, t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ by the same argument as Lemma 2.1. The $\mathrm{hi}$-dwell hypothesis constrains $p$ before $t_0$ but does not constrain the delivery schedule after $t_0$; hence it does not contract the blind window. What does contract the window is a hypothesis on the failure duration (so that the $\mathrm{lo}$ payload is delivered before restoration), not on the $\mathrm{hi}$-duration. $\square$

Lemma E.3 ($H_3$ as minimum failure duration). Under $H_3'$: every $\mathrm{lo}$-interval has length $\geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. Then the controller necessarily receives a $\mathrm{lo}$ payload before restoration, so $u = \mathrm{safe}$ on the entire failure after $t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and on the entire recovery phase $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$. The Phase-III rate is uniformly $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}} > 0$.

Proof. Failure duration $\geq \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ implies the controller learns of the failure at $t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} \leq t_0+\tau$, strictly before restoration. The restoration payload is dispatched at $t_0+\tau \geq t_0+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ and delivered at $t_0+\tau+d$ with $d \leq \delta_{\max}$; the controller holds $\mathrm{safe}$ until then, and on the entire recovery interval $[t_0+\tau, t_0+\tau+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$ either the restoration has not been received (so $u = \mathrm{safe}$) or it has (so $u = \mathrm{normal}$, and the rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{nom}} \geq 0$). In either case the buffer is non-decreasing. The uniform rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ is the worst case and is realized when the restoration payload is not received within the first $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ ticks of recovery. $\square$

