Appendix F. Authority and Preservation

F.1 Recoverable set

Definition F.1 (Viability kernel, discrete-time instance). Let $X = [B_{\min}, B_{\max}]$ and let $f(x, u, p)$ be the successor map of the buffer under control $u \in \{\mathrm{normal},\mathrm{safe}\}$ and power $p \in \{\mathrm{hi},\mathrm{lo}\}$, subject to $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$. The viability kernel $\mathrm{Viab}(X)$ is the largest set $K \subseteq X$ such that for every $x \in K$ there exists a control policy $\pi$ (possibly depending on the full observation history) such that the closed loop remains in $K$ for all time.

Lemma F.2 ($\mathcal{R}$ is a viability subset). Under $H_1$, $\mathcal{R} = [\Phi, B_{\max}]$ is a subset of the viability kernel of $X$ under $\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}$, provided the mission horizon is bounded by $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. Under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$ with (8), $\mathcal{R}$ is a subset of the viability kernel for all horizons.

Proof. A set $K$ is a viability subset if for every $x \in K$ and every admissible disturbance, the closed loop under the chosen control remains in $K$; here "$\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}$" is the control, and the disturbance is the power trace. By Theorem 4.2, $B_0 \in \mathcal{R}$ implies $B(t) \geq B_{\min}$ on the horizon; by construction $B(t) \leq B_{\max}$ (capacity, §1), so $B(t) \in \mathcal{R}$. The set $\mathcal{R}$ is an invariant of the closed loop. Under $G_P^{\mathrm{bb}}$ with (8), the same holds for all horizons by Theorem 4.4. $\square$

Lemma F.3 (Budget compatibility at $\mathcal{R}$). If $\Delta_a \geq 0$ is such that for every $s$ with $B(s) \in \mathcal{R}$ and every $s' = s \xrightarrow{a} s'$, either $B(s') \in \mathcal{R}$ or $B(s') \geq B(s) - \Delta_a$, then for every finite set $A$ with $\sum_{a \in A} \Delta_a \leq B(s) - B_{\min}$, the post-execution state $s' = s \xrightarrow{A} s'$ satisfies $B(s') \geq B_{\min}$.

Proof. By induction on $|A|$. Base: $|A|=0$, $s'=s \in \mathcal{R}$, $B(s') \geq B_{\min}$. Step: suppose the claim holds for $|A|=k-1$ and consider $A$ with $|A|=k$. Pick $a \in A$; let $A_{-a} = A \setminus \{a\}$ and $s_1 = s \xrightarrow{a} s_1$. Either $B(s_1) \in \mathcal{R}$ (so $B(s_1) \geq B_{\min}$ and by the induction hypothesis applied to $s_1$ and $A_{-a}$, with budget $\sum_{a' \in A_{-a}} \Delta_{a'} \leq B(s_1) - B_{\min}$ which holds since $B(s_1) \geq B(s) - \Delta_a$ and $\sum_A \Delta_a \leq B(s) - B_{\min}$), or $B(s_1) \geq B(s) - \Delta_a \geq B_{\min} + \sum_{a' \in A_{-a}} \Delta_{a'}$, and the induction hypothesis applies. $\square$

F.2 Preservation

Theorem 8.1 (restated). Let $H_1$ hold, let $\mathcal{R} = [\Phi, B_{\max}]$, and let $\Pi$ satisfy $\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{Auth}}$ relative to $\mathcal{R}$ with budgets $(\Delta_a)$ as in Definition 7.3. Then every compositionally admissible trace under $\Pi$ preserves $\mathcal{I}$ on $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$.

Proof. Induction on $t \in [0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}]$.

Base ($t=0$). $B(0) = B_0 \in \mathcal{R}$ by $H_1$; $\mathcal{R} \subseteq [B_{\min}, B_{\max}]$, so $B(0) \geq B_{\min}$.

Step. Suppose $B(t) \in \mathcal{R}$. Two cases.

Case (i): no authority decision at $t$; the baseline policy $\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}$ is in force. By Lemma F.2, $B(t+1) \in \mathcal{R}$ (i.e., $B(t+1) \geq B_{\min}$) provided $t+1 \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$. ✓

Case (ii): an authority decision at $t$, with proposed concurrent set $A = A(t)$. By $\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{Auth}}$ (A2), the policy resolves $A$ to $A' = A'(t)$. By (A1), either $B(t \xrightarrow{A'} t+1) \in \mathcal{R}$ (so $B(t+1) \geq B_{\min}$), or $B(t \xrightarrow{A'} t+1) \geq B(t) - \sum_{a \in A'} \Delta_a$, and the baseline controller can restore $B$ to $\mathcal{R}$ at $t+1$. In the latter case, Lemma F.3 applies at $t$ to give $B(t+1) \geq B_{\min}$; and the baseline controller's restoration from $t+1$ onward is governed by Lemma F.2, which applies at $t+1 \leq T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}+\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ to give $B(t+2) \in \mathcal{R}$, etc.

In both cases $B(t+1) \geq B_{\min}$. $\square$

Remark F.4. The induction step does not require the set of concurrent decisions to shrink; the safety obligation is discharged at each step by the recoverable-set inclusion, independently of the size of the set. This closes the "smaller $k$" gap identified in the review. The reduction of $A$ to $A'$ is a property of the policy, not of the induction; the induction only uses (A1), which is a property of the executed set at each step.

