Appendix G. Precedence Axioms

Theorem 9.2 (restated). Under $A_{\mathrm{nom}}$, there exists a system satisfying $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}} \wedge G_O \wedge G_{\mathrm{ch}}$ and $H_1$ in which every agent executes only legitimate actions and $\mathcal{I}$ is violated.

Proof. Two agents $M$ (local, blind window $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$) and $E$ (remote, blind window $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}^E > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$). Set $B_0 = \Phi$, $p(t) = \mathrm{lo}$ on $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$, $p(t) = \mathrm{hi}$ elsewhere. The remote channel has delay and loss such that the blind window exceeds the failure; this requires (C1)–(C3) to be satisfiable with $\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}^E > T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$, which is an architectural assumption on $E$'s communication link.

$M$ observes the failure at $t = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ (Lemma 2.1) and commands $\mathrm{safe}$ on $[\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$; $E$ observes $\mathrm{hi}$ throughout $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$ and commands $\mathrm{normal}$. Under $A_{\mathrm{nom}}$, the composed command is $\mathrm{normal}$ on $[\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$. Consumption: $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ on $[0, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$. Buffer at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$:
B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = \Phi - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} = B_{\min} + \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} + (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} - T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} c_{\mathrm{nom}}


= B_{\min} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})(c_{\mathrm{nom}}-c_{\mathrm{safe}}) < B_{\min}


since $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ (if $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$, the second term of $\Phi$ vanishes and $B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_{\min}$; take $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}} > \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ to violate strictly). Legitimacy: each agent's action satisfies (9) at its own observed state. $\square$

Theorem 9.4 (restated). Under $A_{\mathrm{safe}}$, the configuration of Theorem 9.2 does not violate $\mathcal{I}$.

Proof. Under $A_{\mathrm{safe}}$, the composed command on $[\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$ is $\mathrm{safe}$ (since $M$ commands $\mathrm{safe}$ and $E$ commands $\mathrm{normal}$, and $c_{\mathrm{safe}} < c_{\mathrm{nom}}$, the safe-precedence rule selects $\mathrm{safe}$). Consumption: $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ on $[0, \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}})$, $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ on $[\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}, T_{\mathrm{rec,max}})$. Buffer at $T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}$:
B(T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}) = B_0 - \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}} c_{\mathrm{nom}} - (T_{\mathrm{rec,max}}-\Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}) c_{\mathrm{safe}} \geq \Phi - (\Phi - B_{\min}) = B_{\min}.


✓ $\square$

Lemma G.1 (General resolution functions). Let $\rho : 2^{\mathrm{Act}} \to \mathrm{Act}$ be a resolution function. If $\rho$ satisfies $C(\rho(A)) \leq \max_{u \in A} C(u)$ for every $A$ with $c_{\mathrm{safe}} < c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ (i.e., $\rho$ never selects a command that consumes more than the maximum of the proposed set), then the safety argument of Theorem 9.4 extends: in any configuration with $M$ commanding $\mathrm{safe}$ and $E$ commanding $\mathrm{normal}$, $\rho(\{\mathrm{safe},\mathrm{normal}\}) \in \{\mathrm{safe},\mathrm{normal}\}$ and $C(\rho) \leq c_{\mathrm{nom}}$; if additionally $\rho$ selects $\mathrm{safe}$ whenever it is proposed, then the drain bound of Theorem 9.4 holds. The condition "$\rho$ never exceeds the maximum proposed consumption" is satisfied by both $A_{\mathrm{nom}}$ and $A_{\mathrm{safe}}$; what distinguishes them is whether $\rho$ selects the minimum or the maximum. The safe class of resolution functions is $\{\rho : \rho(A) \in \arg\min_{u \in A} C(u)\}$; the unsafe class includes $A_{\mathrm{nom}} = \arg\max$.

Proof. Direct from the definitions. $\square$

