Appendix H. Policy Independence and the Frontier

Lemma H.1 (Buffer-trigger policy). Let $\pi_{\mathrm{B}}$ be the policy $u(t) = \mathrm{safe}$ iff $B(t) \leq B_{\min}+\varepsilon$, independently of $\hat{p}(t)$. Then under $G_P^{\mathrm{arb}}$ and $G_{\mathrm{ch}}$, the closed loop preserves $\mathcal{I}$ for all horizons provided $B_0 \geq B_{\min}+\varepsilon + c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ and $c_{\mathrm{safe}} < r_o$ (or more precisely, provided the drain at $c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ is dominated by the recovery rate $r_o - c_{\mathrm{safe}} > 0$ at the buffer threshold).

Proof. The trigger signal is $B(t)$, which is directly measurable (it is the quantity being controlled, not an observation of the power). The blind window does not affect the trigger: the policy switches to $\mathrm{safe}$ at the first tick $t$ with $B(t) \leq B_{\min}+\varepsilon$, regardless of whether the controller has observed the failure. On the interval before the switch, drain is at most $c_{\mathrm{nom}}$ per tick; the buffer decreases by at most $c_{\mathrm{nom}} \varepsilon / (c_{\mathrm{nom}} - c_{\mathrm{safe}})$ before the switch, and after the switch the buffer is held at $B_{\min}+\varepsilon$ by a suitable choice of $\varepsilon$ (the standard hysteresis construction). Formally: let $t_s$ be the first tick with $B(t_s) \leq B_{\min}+\varepsilon$. Before $t_s$, $B(t) > B_{\min}+\varepsilon$; at $t_s$, $B(t_s) \geq B_{\min}$ provided $\varepsilon \leq B_0 - B_{\min} - c_{\mathrm{nom}} \cdot 0$ (i.e., $\varepsilon$ small). After $t_s$, $u = \mathrm{safe}$ and $c = c_{\mathrm{safe}}$; if $r_o > c_{\mathrm{safe}}$, the buffer is non-decreasing and the trigger holds; if $r_o \leq c_{\mathrm{safe}}$ and the failure is indefinite, the buffer drains at rate $c_{\mathrm{safe}} - r_o$ and no policy can preserve the invariant indefinitely (this is the unbounded-failure case of Theorem 6.1). For bounded failures, the buffer recovers. $\square$

Remark H.2. The threshold $\Phi$ is derived against $\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}$. The buffer-trigger policy attains $B_0 \geq B_{\min}+\varepsilon$ at the cost of conservative rationing. The pair $(\Phi, \mathrm{utility}(\pi_{\mathrm{naive}}))$ and $(B_{\min}+\varepsilon, \mathrm{utility}(\pi_{\mathrm{B}}))$ are two points on the safety–utility frontier. A policy-independent statement of the preservation theorem would characterize $\min_\pi \Phi_\pi$ over causal policies; in the single-buffer case, the buffer trigger attains the minimum. The multi-buffer case is open.

Lemma H.3 (Network-calculus reading). $\Phi$ is a single-node backlog bound: $\Phi - B_{\min} = $ (arrival-curve latency term) $\times$ (service rate) $+$ (residual service term). Specifically, with arrival curve $\alpha(\Delta t) = r_o \Delta t$ and service curve $\beta(\Delta t) = c_{\mathrm{safe}} \Delta t$, the horizontal deviation at $\Delta t = \Delta_{\mathrm{blind}}$ is the drain, and the backlog bound is the vertical deviation. The multi-buffer extension requires the network-calculus min-plus convolution of the per-buffer arrival and service curves, which is the correct abstraction for the multi-resource problem posed in §11.

Proof. Direct by identification of $\alpha$, $\beta$, and the deviation; standard network-calculus definitions. $\square$

